Pre-R110-198 the validator caught typos, test failures,
secret leaks, and category drift — but NOT the failure
mode that R110-194-B exposed: a topic is defined, a
producer publishes to it, but no consumer exists.
Messages sit in pending.ndjson forever and the
producer is a dead end.

Check 21 closes that gap.  It scans tools/*.py and
recipe/ for mq.enqueue() call-sites (both string-literal
first args AND *_TOPIC module-level constants), then for
each unique producer topic verifies a caller-chain exists:

  (a) workflow recipe (recipe/wf_*.yaml) that references
      the topic in --topic= or hardcoded mq.consume(...)
  (b) sub_recipe (recipe/sub/sub_mas-*.yaml) that mentions
      the topic in its description
  (c) test file (tests/test_*.py) that calls
      mq.consume(<topic>) or mq.enqueue(<topic>, ...)
  (d) tools/dev_mq_consumer*.py hardcoded topic

The check is bash + grep (not python AST) because the
call-sites are widely styled — single-line, multi-line
with arg on next line, _mq.enqueue(variable, ...), etc.
A python AST approach was the first draft but it was
overkill for what is effectively a string-presence
check; the bash version is debuggable with `bash -x`
and runs in <0.1s.
